\fs96 \cf2 GPU debugging\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can debug Mojo code running on GPUs using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-gdb/index.html#"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 CUDA-GDB}}. You can either debug using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 cuda-gdb
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  command-line interface, or through VS Code, using the Mojo and NVIDIA extensions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 Limitations\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb13 Currently there are a couple of notable limitations to debugging Mojo code on GPU:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa127\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
GPU debugging is supported only on NVIDIA GPUs.\cb1 \
\ls4\ilvl0\cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
You cannot debug Mojo code running inside a MAX {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/max/develop/custom-ops/"}}{\fldrslt \ul custom operation}}. (You can only debug Mojo GPU code launched from a Mojo program when using the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/"}}{\fldrslt \ul gpu.host}} API).\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 GPU debugging setup\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To debug Mojo code on GPU, first make sure you have a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/requirements/#gpu-compatibility"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 compatible GPU}}.\
If you're using VS Code, you can run it on the same system where your GPU code is running (the "target system"), or on a separate system using remote debugging.\
To debug on GPU, you need to choose to debug with CUDA-GDB when you start a debugging session. Note that CUDA-GDB has very limited debugging capabilities for Mojo code running on the CPU.\
To set up for GPU debugging:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Install the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-toolkit"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 NVIDIA CUDA Toolkit}} 12.4 or later on the target system. Make sure that the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 cuda-gdb
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  binary is in your 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 $PATH
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  environment variable. For example, if you have CUDA Toolkit 12.8 installed, add 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 /usr/local/cuda12.8/bin
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to your 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 $PATH
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If using VS Code, install {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=nvidia.nsight-vscode-edition"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Nsight Visual Studio Code Edition}} and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=modular-mojotools.vscode-mojo"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Mojo extension}} from the Visual Studio Marketplace.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Using the classic debugger backend\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 CUDA-GDB includes two debugger backends, called the "classic debugger" and the "universal debugger." By default, Mojo uses the CUDA-GDB universal debugger. However, some systems require the classic debugger, instead. If you find that the debugger is losing its connection with the process being debugged, you may need to use the classic debugger. To use the classic debugger backend:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
On the target system, set the environment variable 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 CUDBG_USE_LEGACY_DEBUGGER
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 1
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in your shell configuration file (for example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 .bashrc
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 .zshrc
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 config.fish
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ). Source the file or start a new shell.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
When creating a launch configuration for GPU debugging, add the following settings to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 launch.json
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  configuration:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 "legacyDebugger":\cf15  \cf16 true\cf5 ,\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf5 \cb14 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
"initCommands":\cf15  \cf5 [\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf15 \cb14 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
    \cf17 "set environment CUDBG_USE_LEGACY_DEBUGGER=1"\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf5 \cb14 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
],\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf15 \cb14 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf18 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf19 \uc0\u8232 \cf18 \uc0\u8232 \cf15 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Start GPU debugging from the command line\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To start a GPU debugging session in VS Code from the command line, run the following command on the target system:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf15 \cb14 mojo debug --cuda-gdb --break-on-launch \cf16 --vscode\cf15  myproject.mojo\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 To use the CUDA-GDB command line debugger, omit the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 --vscode
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 --break-on-launch
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  flag is optional but very useful: it stops execution as soon as the GPU kernel launches, allowing you to set breakpoints inside the GPU code.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Start GPU debugging from VS Code\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The easiest way to start GPU debugging from VS Code is to add a \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Launch configuration\cf2 \ulnone . For example, the following launch configuration starts debugging the current Mojo file using CUDA-GDB.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf15 \cb14   \cf5 \{\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 "type":\cf15  \cf17 "mojo-cuda-gdb"\cf5 ,\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 "request":\cf15  \cf17 "launch"\cf5 ,\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 "name":\cf15  \cf17 "Mojo: Debug current Mojo file with CUDA-GDB"\cf5 ,\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 "description":\cf15  \cf17 "Launch and debug the Mojo file that is active on the editor when the debug session starts, using CUDA-GDB."\cf5 ,\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 "mojoFile":\cf15  \cf17 "$\{file\}"\cf5 ,\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 "args":\cf15  \cf5 [],\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 "env":\cf15  \cf5 [],\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 "cwd":\cf15  \cf17 "$\{workspaceFolder\}"\cf5 ,\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 "breakOnLaunch":\cf15  \cf16 true\cf5 ,\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 "legacyDebugger":\cf15  \cf16 true\cf5 ,\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 "initCommands":\cf15  \cf5 [\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf17 "set environment CUDBG_USE_LEGACY_DEBUGGER=1"\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 ],\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14   \cf5 \},\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The last two settings, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 legacyDebugger
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 initCommands
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  should only be included if required on your system to maintain a stable connection to the process being debugged, as described in \cf5 \ul Using the classic debugger backend\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Issuing CUDA-GDB commands\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Some features of the debugger are only available via CUDA-GDB commands, so it's worth familiarizing yourself with CUDA-GDB even if you're using VS Code as a frontend.\
If you're running in VS Code, you can enter CUDA-GDB commands in the debug console by prefixing them with a single backtick (`). Note that the console may automatically add a second backtick at the end of your command, which prevents it from being recognized as a CUDA-GDB command. Be sure to remove the second backtick before submitting the command.\
The examples in the following section are raw CUDA-GDB commands, without the backtick.\
A good starting point for learning about CUDA-GDB is the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-gdb/index.html#"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul CUDA-GDB User Manual}}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Tips and tricks\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The following sections provide tips for some common tasks in GPU debugging.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Setting breakpoints in GPU code\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 There are a few quirks to setting breakpoints in GPU code. If you set a breakpoint in GPU code before the GPU kernel launches, the breakpoint will show up in a different function (a CPU function).\
When the debugger pauses at this first breakpoint, click Continue to resume execution, and the debugger should stop at the correct location in the GPU code. When paused at the first breakpoint, you can add more breakpoints in the GPU code, however, the breakpoints won't show up in the left gutter until the GPU kernel launches.\
Symbol breakpoints aren't supported when debugging with CUDA-GDB.\
On the CUDA-GDB command line, you can set a breakpoint using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 break
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  command (which can be abbreviated to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 b
